5:[["$","$L10","4.4",{"topic":{"id":"4.4","title":"Forensic Imaging and Data Acquisition","minutes":16,"summary":"Create a documented representation of the source, verify what you acquired, and explain any limits on completeness or repeatability.","sections":[{"title":"Image formats and source scope","body":["A raw bit-stream image represents acquired sectors in sequence. Formats such as E01 can add metadata, compression, segmentation, and integrity checks; Ex01 extends the format family. The file extension alone does not tell you what was acquired. Record the source device, range, sector size, hidden areas considered, tool, settings, and any inaccessible regions.","A logical collection of files differs from a physical image of storage. The former may omit deleted content and filesystem structures; the latter may still omit host-protected areas or inaccessible flash translation layers. State the scope precisely. For mobile and cloud sources, use the method appropriate to the service rather than forcing a disk-image analogy."]},{"title":"Acquisition and hash workflow","body":["Prepare and verify destination storage, use a suitable write-protection method, and record identifiers before acquisition. Calculate and retain the source or acquired-data hash as the method permits. Record bad sectors, read errors, retries, and interruptions. After acquisition, verify the image or extracted stream according to the format and document exactly what the hash covers.","A container hash can differ from the underlying data hash because metadata or compression changed. Do not compare unrelated values and call the result a failure. For a live source that continues changing, explain why a stable full-source hash may be unavailable and what integrity checks support the captured material."]},{"title":"Working copies and quality control","body":["Preserve the master image under controlled access and examine a verified working copy. Record every derived export and its relationship to the parent image. If the image format is converted, verify the underlying data where possible and preserve the original container. A successful copy operation is not enough without an integrity check.","Quality control includes capacity planning, tool validation, error review, and a second-person check of identifiers. A hash collision is not a practical substitute for explaining a mismatch caused by the wrong source or altered output. The acquisition report should let a reviewer understand both the technical result and its evidential limits."]}],"practice":{"title":"Plan a 500 GB acquisition","scenario":"A laptop disk contains bad sectors, and the available destination is nearly full.","steps":["Confirm source and destination capacity and identifiers.","Choose a format and error-handling strategy appropriate to the tool.","Record acquisition hashes, errors, and inaccessible ranges.","Verify a working copy and preserve the master before analysis."],"debrief":"An acquisition with documented read errors may still be useful, but the missing ranges must be visible in the report. Silent omission is not acceptable."},"terms":[],"lawIds":["bsa","bnss"],"sources":["cert","nist-guide","bsa"],"labId":"hash","unit":4,"objectives":["Explain image formats and source scope and apply it to an investigation.","Explain acquisition and hash workflow and apply it to an investigation.","Explain working copies and quality control and apply it to an investigation."],"question":{"id":"q-4.4","prompt":"Why distinguish an image-container hash from an underlying-data hash?","options":["They may cover different bytes and metadata.","They always prove different crimes.","One is not a cryptographic value.","The distinction is irrelevant to verification."],"answer":0,"explanation":"A valid comparison must use the same representation and scope; container metadata can change its hash.","unit":4}}}]]
